  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...This afternoon, a cold front extends from \nwestern VA to middle TN. Meanwhile, a mostly stationary \nprefrontal trough extends from the Tidewater region of VA to \nwestern NC. The prefrontal trough has been convectively active \nall day today thanks to a persistent SW LLJ feeding an influx of\nmoisture into the boundary. While the LLJ is forecast to weaken\nsome, an area of deep moisture convergence should remain in \nplace along that trough through the night, favoring multiple \nrounds of convection.\n\nOf note, a small SW to NE oriented linear convective feature \nhas recently developed around the higher terrain of NW NC, and \nis moving east. The airmass downstream of this line should \nsupport a continuation across northern NC, aided by modest WAA \nand a continued influx of moisture within SW low-level flow. It \nmay encounter a more deeply mixed, and more capped, airmass \nacross central and eastern NC, but could potentially hold \ntogether in some form or fashion across the far northern reaches\nof ENC after sunset this evening with a gusty wind risk (40-50 \nmph). \n\nLater this evening through early Thursday morning, low-level \nconvergence should gradually shift south from VA into northern \nNC as the above-mentioned front and pre-frontal trough more or \nless merge. The front is then expected to stall across ENC \nduring the day on Thursday, and may not push offshore until \nsometime on Saturday.\n\nA plume of anomalous, and deep, moisture is forecast to reside \nalong the stalled frontal zone, with lift aided by periods of \nWAA and large-scale support from passing shortwaves. Rainfall \nrates will be aided by moderate instability through at least \nFriday, then instability begins to wane after that. The overall \nsynoptic pattern supports periods of heavy rain and excessive \nrainfall rates with an accompanying risk of flooding and flash \nflooding. This is also supported by various forecast aids, \nincluding the GEFS and ECMWF EFI, which show a strong signal for\nheavy rain over the area.\n\nPerhaps the main complicating factor is where the strongest \nlow- level forcing will reside as this is likely to be where the\nthe most intense rainfall rates will occur. The latest guidance\nhas trended towards the strongest convergence and most intense \nrates shifting south a bit quicker during the day Thursday, \nwhich would then setup more of a widespread moderate overrunning\nrainfall setup from Thursday night into Friday night. While \nthis would still support notable rainfall totals, a faster shift\nsouth would tend to lower rainfall totals some thanks to a \nshorter residence time of the most intense rates. In light of \nthis, guidance have trended down slightly with rainfall totals. \nThis doesn't change the messaging much, but the Flood Watch will\nnow reflect 3-5\" vs 4-6\". There is still some potential for a \nfew areas to get up to 5-8\" where those most intense rates last \nthe longest. \n\nWhile heavy rain and flooding is the primary risk, there may be\njust enough shear and instability to support a marginal severe \nthunderstorm risk through Friday. Deep, moist profiles should \ntend to favor water-loaded downdrafts, but some enhanced low- \nlevel shear may support a brief/weak tornado.",
